  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n- Rain chances remain through Wednesday night for southern areas\n\nMost areas saw at least one round of at least showers with scattered \nstorms at some point today. The main area of rain earlier was the \nremnants of a 60 knot low level jet that moved over the area very \nearly this morning. Another secondary weaker low level jet of 35 \nknots is supporting the showers exiting the SE corner of the area. \nThe remainder of the rain is dissipating as we see the wave and low \nlevel jet forcing moving out, and weak short wave ridging building \nin.\n\nMost areas will see little to no additional rain tonight. The \nexception to this will be areas across the far southern portion of \nthe area overnight. The sfc front is sinking south, and another low \nlevel jet will intersect it. This will even be a bit weaker, but \njust enough forcing given the deep moisture in place. MU CAPEs are \nforecast to increase to a few hundred J/kg with the arrival of the \nlow level jet. This instability will be elevated, so the potential \nof severe weather is quite limited.\n\nAnother low level jet will clip the far southern portion of the area \nagain Wednesday night, bring additional shower/storm chances. There \nwas a fair amount of disagreement with rain placement Wednesday \nnight. A better consensus has developed, with the nose of the low \nlevel jet likely along I-94, and bringing rain mostly south of I-96. \nOnce again, instability will be elevated being further removed from \nthe sfc front to our south. Some localized heavy rain will be the \nmain threat.\n\n- Cooler and drier weather Thursday and Friday\n\nOnce the low level jet Wednesday night passes, the front will be \ndriven further south away from the area. In addition, we will be \nseeing a stronger system start to become organized well west of the \narea. This will temporarily stop the weak impulses coming through, \nand just allowing some ridging to reside for a couple of days. The \nlack of rain and breaks in the clouds will allow for some sun to \nwarm max temps up a couple of degrees compared to the cooler weather \nunder the clouds and rain. Temperatures will be quite seasonable \nwith highs well into the 70s, and lows in the 50s.\n\n- Stronger system with heavy rain potential Saturday\n\nThe better organized system will start affecting the area late \nFriday with a strengthening flow ahead of it from the SW. The \natmosphere should saturate sufficiently late Friday night to allow \nrain to begin to reach the ground. \n\nThe best chance of rain will come centered on Saturday as the low \nlevel jet axis noses over the area in a weakening state. The warm \nsector of this system stays south of the area as the low moves \nthrough the heart of the area. The vast majority of the instability \nwill stay south of the state border. There may be a chance of some \nthunder across the far southern counties. \n\nThe most likely highest impact with this system will be heavy rain \npotential. The vast amount of moisture that will be associated with \nthis system and the strength and slow speed, will all for some \nheavy rain amounts to be possible. WPC has our area placed under a \nSlight risk for Saturday, which we agree with strongly at this time.\n\nThings become quite uncertain for Sunday and beyond. We will see the \npattern reload with an upper low to our west, bringing a deep SW \nflow over the area. This will eject short wave troughs at times, and \ninteract with higher moisture. The question is where does the main \naxis set up. It is too far to pin down those details at this time, \nso we will continue to monitor the trends.",
